{
 "Q1 swap rule gives q-binomials": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "coefficient of x^k y^(n-k) in (x+y)^n is the Gauss q-binomial for every n <= 8 (exact)"
 },
 "Q2 symmetry's q = swap rule's q": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "E and F keep y x - q x y = 0 only when the symmetry's parameter p solves [{p: q}] (exact)"
 },
 "Q3 U_q(sl2) relations": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "K E = q^2 E K and E F - F E = (K - 1/K)/(q - 1/q) hold exactly on degrees 1..6 (spins 1/2..3)"
 },
 "Q4 recoupling = q-6j": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "342 recoupling numbers built only from the plane and the twisted product rule (q = 1.3) match sqrt([2j12+1][2j23+1])|q-6j| to 5.6e-16"
 },
 "Q5 orthogonality": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "sum_x [2x+1][2f+1]{a b x; c d f}{a b x; c d g} = delta_fg for q = 1.3 and q = e^(i pi/r), r = 3..7"
 },
 "Q6 pentagon identity": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "Biedenharn-Elliott holds for real q and at roots of unity with the root-of-unity cut on faces, and fails without that cut"
 },
 "Q7 Pachner 2-3 move": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "two tetrahedra = sum over the new edge of three tetrahedra, at every tested root of unity"
 },
 "Q8 Pachner 1-4 move": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "one tetrahedron = (1/w^2) sum over four new edges of four tetrahedra; the factor is forced to be w^2 = sum of [2j+1]^2 = r/(2 sin^2(pi/r))"
 },
 "Q9 same answer on every triangulation": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "Z(3-sphere) is the same, 1/w^2, for 2, 5, 8 and 9 tetrahedra (a 1-4 move, another 1-4 move, then a 2-3 move), r = 3, 4, 5, 6"
 },
 "Q10 the root of unity is what makes the sum finite": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "quantum dimensions are the even terms of our dial's own sequence; it first hits zero at step 2r, so [2j+1] first vanishes at 2j+1 = r and the labels stop; as q -> 1 (r -> infinity) the vertex weight w^2 diverges like r^3 (the Ponzano-Regge infinity)"
 },
 "Q11 sign of d - k^2/4 = sign of Lambda": {
  "pass": true,
  "detail": "after the ruler change d' = d - k^2/4; roots of unity sit on the sphere side (d' = sin^2(pi/2r) > 0, Lambda > 0), q = 1 on the flat line (Ponzano-Regge, Lambda = 0), real q on the hyperbolic side (Lambda < 0); d' scales as 1/r^2 like Lambda (the constant in front depends on the length unit, so only the scaling is claimed)"
 },
